# Chord Melody: Jazz Ballad with Reharmonisation (repertoire plan: st-chord-melody-2). An
# original 32-bar AABA ballad in F major for solo guitar. The melody is always the top note;
# on beats 1 and 3 a chord grip sits under it, between them the melody moves alone.
# The first A (bars 1-8) uses the plain chords: Fmaj7, Dm7, Gm7, C7. The second A (bars 9-16)
# plays the same tune but changes Dm7 to D7, the secondary dominant of Gm7: one finger moves,
# F up to F# on string 3. The bridge (bars 17-24) walks through three secondary dominants,
# each a dominant seventh a fifth above the chord it leads to: A7 to Dm7, D7 to Gm7, and G7 to
# C7 by way of Gm7 (II7 - ii7 - V7). The last A (bars 25-32) keeps the D7s and, in bar 31,
# replaces C7 with Gb7, its tritone substitute: the same B flat and E on top (the 7th and 3rd
# of C7 are the 3rd and 7th of Gb7), with the bass moving down by half steps, G - Gb - F.
# The melody notes on beats 1 and 3 are chord tones or common tones of both harmonies (in
# bars 2 and 6 it uses only D, C and A, which belong to Dm7 and D7 alike).
# Positions: VIII and X, with open A for the A7 bass. Each chord symbol shows the grip.
title: Chord Melody: Jazz Ballad with Reharmonisation
composer: Sixstring Academy
source: Original chord-melody ballad
licence: Original study for Sixstring Academy
difficulty: 8
tags: chord melody, jazz, ballad, reharmonisation, secondary dominants, tritone substitution
tempo: 60
time: 4/4
key: F

